WEBVTT
TITLE Linear Bounded Automata and the Context-Sensitive Class: the Bounded Tape, the Configuration Count and the Decidability It Buys — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Six classes stand in one line: regular, deterministic context-free, context-free, context-sensitive, recursive, recursively enumerable.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Three lessons already stopped at context-free. Between context-free and recursive sits one rung, and it has an automaton of its own.

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
The automaton is a Turing machine that may not use more tape than the input occupies. It is called a linear bounded automaton.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Its languages are exactly the context-sensitive ones, proved by Kuroda in 1964, and every one of them is decidable.

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
This page computes the automaton, the bound that makes it decide, the grammar that matches it, and the decision table that surprises people.

6
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.000
A linear bounded automaton is a seven-tuple: states, input alphabet, tape alphabet, transition function, start state, accept state, reject state.

7
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Two symbols of the tape alphabet are endmarkers. No transition may print over them, and no transition may move past them.

8
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.000
So on a string of n letters the tape is exactly n + 2 cells: the input, and one marker at each end. Nothing else exists.

9
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.000
The machine on this page uses 7 states and 8 tape symbols: a, b, c, three working marks X, Y, Z, and the two endmarkers.

10
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Its language is a to the n, b to the n, c to the n: the standard first language that no push-down automaton can accept.

11
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.000
The machine crosses the tape three times per round. First it marks one a with X and walks right to the first b.

12
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Then it marks that b with Y, walks right to the first c, marks it with Z, and walks all the way back to the left marker.

13
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Two rounds on aabbcc turn the tape into X X Y Y Z Z. The third round finds no a, and only checks that nothing is left unmarked.

14
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.000
It accepts in 29 moves. On abc it takes 10; on aaabbbccc, 58; on aaaabbbbcccc, 97.

15
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.000
The head never left the eight cells it started with. Every write was inside the input plus the two markers.

16
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.000
A configuration is the state, the tape contents and the head position. Nothing else about the machine can change.

17
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.000
The count is states times head positions times tape contents: 7 times m + 2, times 8 to the power m.

18
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.000
An input of 1 letter gives 168 configurations. Four letters, 172,032. Six letters, 14,680,064, which is enormous and still finite.

19
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.000
Finite is the whole point. A machine that can only pass through finitely many configurations either accepts, or repeats one and loops forever.

20
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.000
That sentence is the proof that every context-sensitive language is decidable, and it is why this rung sits below recursive.

21
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Here is a machine that never stops: two states, and a head that walks right then left forever. It repeats its first configuration after 2 moves.

22
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Simulating it tells you nothing, because the simulation never ends. Counting does.

23
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:18.000
Run it for at most 7 times m + 2, times 8 to the power m moves: 168 on a one-letter input. If it has not accepted by then, it never will.

24
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.000
That bound is the decider. It is a different machine that watches the first one, and it always halts with an answer.

25
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.000
So acceptance for this automaton is decidable. The trap is to conclude that everything about it is.

26
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Draw a dot for every configuration and an arrow for every move. Acceptance is reachability of an accepting dot.

27
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.000
This machine is deterministic, so its dots form one path: 11 configurations at n = 1, 30 at n = 2, 59 at n = 3, 98 at n = 4.

28
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:48.000
A nondeterministic one branches. A machine that may write a or b over each a reaches 5, 11, 23 and 47 configurations for 1 to 4 letters.

29
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:54.000
Those counts are 3 times 2 to the k, minus 1: 2 to the k + 1 minus 1 running configurations, plus 2 to the k accepting ones.

30
00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Small or large, the set is finite, and that is all the decidability argument needs.

31
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.000
The grammar side is shorter. Four rules: S goes to abc, S goes to a S B c, c B goes to B c, b B goes to b b.

32
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.000
No rule makes a string shorter, so no sentential form is ever longer than the word it will become. That is a non-contracting grammar.

33
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:18.000
Searching every sentential form up to length 9 finds 17 forms and exactly 3 terminal strings: abc, aabbcc, aaabbbccc.

34
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.000
That set equals the language restricted to length 9, computed independently by a membership test on all 29,523 strings.

35
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Non-contracting grammars generate exactly the context-sensitive languages. The automaton and the grammar name the same class.

36
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.000
Union, intersection, complement, concatenation, Kleene star, reversal: the class is closed under all six.

37
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.000
Complement is the surprise. Context-free languages are not closed under complement; context-sensitive ones are.

38
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.000
That is the second LBA problem, open from 1964 and settled in 1988 by Immerman and Szelepcsényi: nondeterministic space is closed under complement.

39
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.000
Homomorphism is the exception. A homomorphism that erases letters can shrink a word, so it can leave the class; the ones that never erase stay inside.

40
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.000
And determinism is still open: nobody knows whether deterministic linear bounded automata accept fewer languages.

41
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.000
Membership is decidable, but not cheaply: it is complete for polynomial space, so no polynomial-time algorithm is known.

42
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:12.000
Emptiness is not decidable at all. Given a context-sensitive grammar, no algorithm decides whether its language is empty.

43
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:18.000
Equivalence, finiteness and universality fail the same way. Complement closure does not rescue any of them.

44
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:24.000
Compare the rung below: context-free emptiness is decidable, and equivalence is not. The surprises sit in different places.

45
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:30.000
Keep the two questions apart. Is this word in the language is decidable; is this language empty is not.

46
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Shape one: place a language in the hierarchy. Count the independent equalities: three need linear space, two need a stack, one needs nothing.

47
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:42.000
Shape two: is the statement true? Every context-sensitive language is recursive; there is a recursive language that is not context-sensitive. Both are true.

48
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:48.000
Shape three: which automaton accepts this class? The linear bounded automaton, with a tape of exactly the input plus two markers.

49
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:54.000
Shape four: decidable or not? Membership yes, emptiness no. Say which question is being asked before answering.

50
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.000
One line to carry: the tape is bounded, so the configurations are finite, so membership is decidable. Nothing else follows.

